      "description": "The pilot’s choice to publish translated landing content and summaries while leaving full interview chapters in Dutch created a discoverability and navigation problem that readers already reported. The strongest story is a focused usability lesson: partial multilingual access can raise reader expectation that the full text is available in their language, and the site’s linking/labeling must compensate for that gap.",
      "audience": "Digital humanities project leads, archivists, UX designers for cultural heritage websites, multilingual content managers",
      "rationale": "Why it matters: Projects that offer selective translations — summaries but not full texts — risk confusing readers and generating negative navigation feedback; addressing this early can shape design and prioritization before scaling translations. Gaps & uncertainty: The evidence is limited (30 invites, 9 feedback-form opens, 4 comments) and no formal usability or comprehension testing was done, so the scale and specifics of the problem beyond initial reports are unknown.",
      "evidence": "Facts from source: the landing page and six short summaries are available in English and Polish while longer interview chapters remain in Dutch; each translated summary links to the original chapter and identifies which text is available in which language; 30 invitation links were sent to local reading groups, 9 people opened the feedback form, and 4 submitted comments about finding their way between summaries and interviews; the announcement asks readers for feedback on navigation."
