    {
      "title": "An incremental, human-translated pilot: North Quay’s staged multilingual rollout keeps full translations contingent on future funding",
      "description": "The project deployed a limited, human-translated surface (landing page and summaries) as a deliberate small-scale pilot rather than releasing automated or full translations, and framed broader translation work as a funding-dependent possibility rather than a promise. The editorial angle is an account of a conservative, resource-aware staged rollout for multilingual access.",
      "audience": "Project managers, grant officers, cultural heritage administrators, teams planning multilingual pilots",
      "rationale": "Why it matters: Other institutions planning multilingual access can learn from a cautious, staged approach that uses paid translators for limited content and solicits navigation feedback before committing resources to full translations. Gaps & uncertainty: The source does not state the project’s motivations (budget, quality concerns, or policy), nor whether other options (machine translation) were considered; it only records that two paid translators were used and that broader translations are a possibility for a later funding application.",
      "evidence": "Facts from source: the project worked with two paid translators and the published account does not describe an automated translation system; landing page and six summaries are translated while longer chapters remain in Dutch; the announcement frames further languages and full translated chapters as possibilities for a later funding application rather than promised additions."
    },
    {
      "title": "Early feedback metrics show engagement but weak sample size—evaluation for a multilingual pilot remains preliminary",
      "description": "The pilot collected limited interaction and feedback data (invite opens and a small number of navigation comments) but did not run any reading-comprehension or language-comparison tasks. The story is an evidence-based assessment: initial engagement exists, yet the project lacks the evaluative measures needed to judge the multilingual experience.",
      "audience": "Researchers evaluating pilot studies, funders reviewing evidence for translation projects, project teams designing evaluation protocols",
      "rationale": "Why it matters: Funders and teams need clear, measurable evaluation plans to decide whether to scale multilingual access. The pilot’s initial metrics are too sparse to justify conclusions about reader comprehension or language preference. Gaps & uncertainty: The sample is tiny (30 invites, 9 opens, 4 comments); there is no systematic testing (no comprehension tasks or language-comparison studies) and no demographic or usage breakdown provided.",
      "evidence": "Facts from source: thirty invitation links were sent to local reading groups; nine people opened the feedback form, and four submitted comments about navigation; explicitly, no reading-comprehension task or language comparison has been carried out."
    },
    {
      "title": "Transparent labeling of what is translated creates clear reader expectations — but its effectiveness is untested",
      "description": "The pilot explicitly labels which texts are available in which language and links translated summaries to the original Dutch chapters; this transparency is a defensible editorial tactic to manage reader expectations when full translations are unavailable. The piece would examine the practice of explicit language-labeling as a governance and UX tactic.",
      "audience": "Content strategists, web editors, multilingual site UX leads, cultural heritage communicators",
      "rationale": "Why it matters: Clear labeling is a low-cost way to reduce confusion when offering partial translations; documenting and exemplifying this approach helps others adopt similar safeguards. Gaps & uncertainty: The source does not provide user data proving that labeling resolved confusion—indeed, some navigation comments were still received—so the real-world effectiveness and best labeling patterns remain unknown.",
      "evidence": "Facts from source: each translated summary links to the original chapter and identifies which text is available in which language; the announcement explains the limited access now available and asks readers for feedback on navigation."
    },
    {
      "title": "The pilot’s communication framing: requesting navigation feedback rather than promising multilingual expansion",
      "description": "Rather than presenting full multilingual release commitments, the announcement frames translations beyond the pilot as contingent possibilities tied to future funding and explicitly solicits reader feedback on navigation. The editorial idea is an exploration of how cautious public communication can set expectations for experimental releases.",
      "audience": "Communications teams for cultural projects, project leads managing stakeholder expectations, grant writers",
      "rationale": "Why it matters: How a project frames limited availability (requesting feedback, not promising expansion) affects stakeholder expectations and can protect teams from pressure to deliver immediate full translations. Gaps & uncertainty: The source records the wording but does not explain the team’s intention behind this framing or how readers received it beyond the few navigation comments.",
      "evidence": "Facts from source: the current announcement explains the limited access now available and asks readers for feedback on navigation; further languages and full translated chapters are presented as possibilities for a later funding application rather than promised additions."
    }
  ]
